    "image_with_text_Hzd6kr": {
      "type": "image-with-text",
      "blocks": {
        "text_JXFiiL": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>The signs: disappearing jawline, under-eye puffiness, jowling, and facial asymmetry — none caused by aging.</p>",
            "text_font": "body",
            "text_size": "subtext-sm",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_line_height": 1.6,
            "text_letter_spacing": 0,
            "text_capitalize": false,
            "secondary_color": true,
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "text_RqY6DM": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>So you do what everyone does. You buy retinol serums. You try gua sha. You get an ice roller. Maybe you invest in a microcurrent device, or even look into filler. Some of it helps a little, temporarily. But your face always settles right back to where it was. Nothing sticks.</p><p><strong>Here's why: you've been treating your face. But the problem isn't on your face.</strong></p><p>The problem is in your neck. And until you fix it there, nothing you put on your face will make a lasting difference.</p>",
            "text_font": "body",
            "text_size": "subtext-md",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_line_height": 1.6,
            "text_letter_spacing": 0,
            "text_capitalize": false,
            "secondary_color": false,
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "text_3Ntj3D": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><em>The Cause</em></p>",
            "text_font": "body",
            "text_size": "subtext-sm",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_line_height": 1.6,
            "text_letter_spacing": 0,
            "text_capitalize": false,
            "secondary_color": false,
            "highlighted_text": "marker",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "heading_hKKjqh": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "<strong>Your phone is aging your face. Here's the biomechanics.</strong>",
            "heading_size": "title-md",
            "heading_tag": "h2",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle",
            "highlighted_reveal": false
          }
        },
        "text_9JACVt": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>The average American spends over 7 hours a day looking at a screen. Every time you look down at your phone or lean forward at a desk, your head shifts forward of your shoulders. This is called <strong>forward head posture</strong> — and research estimates that 66% to 90% of the population has it.</p><p>That might not sound like a big deal. But here's what's actually happening inside your body:</p><p>Your head weighs about 10 to 12 pounds. When it sits directly over your shoulders, your neck supports that weight efficiently. But for every inch your head moves forward, the effective load on your neck increases dramatically.</p>",
            "text_font": "body",
            "text_size": "subtext-md",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_line_height": 1.6,
            "text_letter_spacing": 0,
            "text_capitalize": false,
            "secondary_color": false,
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "image_YFAJHY": {
          "type": "image",
          "settings": {
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/565.jpg",
            "image_width": "full_width",
            "image_max_width": 1000,
            "image_max_width_mobile": 100
          }
        },
        "text_Ar7Tby": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Hansraj, 2014: At a 45° tilt — the angle you hold your phone — your neck bears nearly 50 lbs of force.</p>",
            "text_font": "body",
            "text_size": "subtext-sm",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_line_height": 1.6,
            "text_letter_spacing": 0,
            "text_capitalize": false,
            "secondary_color": true,
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "text_VWAzff": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><em>A landmark 2014 study published in Surgical Technology International by Dr. Kenneth Hansraj found that at a 45-degree forward tilt — the angle most people hold their phone at — the effective weight on the cervical spine increases to </em><em><strong>49.4 pounds</strong></em><em>.</em></p><p><em>That's nearly 50 pounds of force, pulling on your neck muscles, every time you look at your phone.</em></p>",
            "text_font": "body",
            "text_size": "subtext-md",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_line_height": 1.6,
            "text_letter_spacing": 0,
            "text_capitalize": false,
            "secondary_color": false,
            "highlighted_text": "marker",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "text_FWcmJ3": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Over months and years, this isn't just \"bad posture.\" It physically changes the muscle structure in your neck. And those muscles are directly connected to your face.</p><p><strong>Forward head posture doesn't just make you look slouchy. It changes the way your face looks.</strong> Here's the anatomy that explains why.</p>",
            "text_font": "body",
            "text_size": "subtext-md",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_line_height": 1.6,
            "text_letter_spacing": 0,
            "text_capitalize": false,
            "secondary_color": false,
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        }
      },
