    "rich_text_A9JTJQ": {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "blocks": {
        "text_rY3CXp": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><em>The Mechanism</em></p>",
            "text_font": "body",
            "text_size": "subtext-sm",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_line_height": 1.6,
            "text_letter_spacing": 0,
            "text_capitalize": false,
            "secondary_color": false,
            "highlighted_text": "marker",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "heading_Lg4Lib": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "<strong>Two muscles you've never heard of are reshaping your face right now.</strong>",
            "heading_size": "title-md",
            "heading_tag": "h2",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle",
            "highlighted_reveal": false
          }
        },
        "heading_rk7MTF": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Muscle #1: The SCM (sternocleidomastoid)",
            "heading_size": "title-sm",
            "heading_tag": "h2",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle",
            "highlighted_reveal": false
          }
        },
        "image_MFTXaj": {
          "type": "image",
          "settings": {
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/1488f510894cf3b21d536fd12b54ed6b.jpg",
            "image_width": "full_width",
            "image_max_width": 50,
            "image_max_width_mobile": 50
          }
        },
        "text_RaxEg9": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>The SCM muscle: runs from behind your ear to your collarbone. When locked, it holds your head forward and compresses drainage.</p>",
            "text_font": "body",
            "text_size": "subtext-sm",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_line_height": 1.6,
            "text_letter_spacing": 0,
            "text_capitalize": false,
            "secondary_color": true,
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "text_XzQVBx": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Running from behind your ear down to your collarbone, the SCM is one of the thickest, most powerful muscles in your neck. Its job is to rotate and flex your head. But when you have forward head posture, the SCM gets locked in a shortened, contracted position — sometimes for years without ever fully releasing.</p><p>A locked SCM does two things that directly affect your appearance:</p><p><strong>First, it holds your head in the forward position,</strong> maintaining the postural pattern that causes all the downstream effects on your face.</p><p><strong>Second, it compresses the jugular veins and lymphatic drainage pathways that run alongside it.</strong> These are the channels that drain fluid out of your face. When they're compressed by a tight SCM, the fluid that's supposed to drain downward gets trapped — under your eyes, in your cheeks, along your jawline. You wake up puffy, and it never fully goes away, because the drain is physically being squeezed shut 24 hours a day.</p>",
            "text_font": "body",
            "text_size": "subtext-md",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_line_height": 1.6,
            "text_letter_spacing": 0,
            "text_capitalize": false,
            "secondary_color": false,
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "image_G8Pgqw": {
          "type": "image",
          "settings": {
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/555666.jpg",
            "image_width": "full_width",
            "image_max_width": 50,
            "image_max_width_mobile": 50
          }
        },
        "text_B3cN8r": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Left: Normal drainage flow. Right: Locked SCM compressing veins and lymphatic pathways — trapping fluid in the face.</p>",
            "text_font": "heading",
            "text_size": "subtext-sm",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_line_height": 1.6,
            "text_letter_spacing": 0,
            "text_capitalize": false,
            "secondary_color": true,
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "text_Rtzk3M": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>The SCM has been identified as a key contributor to cervicogenic fluid retention and impaired cranial venous drainage when chronically shortened. — Adapted from clinical postural anatomy literature</p>",
            "text_font": "body",
            "text_size": "subtext-sm",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_line_height": 1.6,
            "text_letter_spacing": 0,
            "text_capitalize": false,
            "secondary_color": true,
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "heading_GbabmY": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Muscle #2: The Platysma",
            "heading_size": "title-sm",
            "heading_tag": "h2",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle",
            "highlighted_reveal": false
          }
        },
        "text_7NMNFR": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>This is the muscle that most people — including most skincare professionals — have never heard of. And it might be the single most important muscle in facial aging.</p>",
            "text_font": "body",
            "text_size": "subtext-md",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_line_height": 1.6,
            "text_letter_spacing": 0,
            "text_capitalize": false,
            "secondary_color": false,
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "image_ALgqEw": {
          "type": "image",
          "settings": {
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/33344.jpg",
            "image_width": "full_width",
            "image_max_width": 50,
            "image_max_width_mobile": 50
          }
        },
        "text_nfw8eW": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><em>The platysma muscle: a thin sheet running from your chest to your jawline. When tight, it pulls your entire lower face down.</em></p>",
            "text_font": "body",
            "text_size": "subtext-sm",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_line_height": 1.6,
            "text_letter_spacing": 0,
            "text_capitalize": false,
            "secondary_color": true,
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "text_UDVwkd": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>The platysma is a thin, broad sheet of muscle that originates on your upper chest and collarbone, runs up through your entire neck, and inserts directly into your jawline and the corners of your mouth. It is physically connected to the structures that define your lower face.</p><p>When your head sits forward and the SCM is locked, the platysma gets pulled into a chronically shortened, tight position. And because it's directly attached to your jawline, <strong>a tight platysma literally pulls your entire lower face downward.</strong></p><p>The result:</p><p>Your jawline loses definition — not because of fat, but because it's being pulled down from below. Jowls form along the jaw as tissue sags under the constant downward tension. Nasolabial folds (smile lines) deepen as the midface gets dragged. Neck bands appear as the platysma fibers become visible through the skin. And because most people's forward head posture isn't perfectly symmetrical, the pull is uneven — creating facial asymmetry that makes one side look different from the other.</p>",
            "text_font": "body",
            "text_size": "subtext-md",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_line_height": 1.6,
            "text_letter_spacing": 0,
            "text_capitalize": false,
            "secondary_color": false,
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "image_dJJBrV": {
          "type": "image",
          "settings": {
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/09090909.jpg",
            "image_width": "full_width",
            "image_max_width": 50,
            "image_max_width_mobile": 50
          }
        },
        "text_w3NxFJ": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><em>The platysma pull: one tight muscle creating jawline loss, jowls, deeper smile lines, neck bands, and facial asymmetry.</em></p>",
            "text_font": "heading",
            "text_size": "subtext-sm",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_line_height": 1.6,
            "text_letter_spacing": 0,
            "text_capitalize": false,
            "secondary_color": true,
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "text_jew7tW": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>The platysma originates from the fascia of the upper chest and inserts into the mandible (jawbone) and the skin of the lower face. Chronic platysma tension is a primary factor in lower face ptosis and the appearance of jowling. — StatPearls, National Library of Medicine (NBK545294)</p><p>Board-certified facial plastic surgeon Dr. Anil Shah has documented how platysma tension \"lowers the eyelids and midface, accentuating malar and nasolabial folds.\" — Shah Facial Plastics</p>",
            "text_font": "body",
            "text_size": "subtext-sm",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_line_height": 1.6,
            "text_letter_spacing": 0,
            "text_capitalize": false,
            "secondary_color": true,
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "text_bLjmrb": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>This is the same muscle that plastic surgeons cut and tighten during a <strong>platysmaplasty — a surgical procedure that costs $5,000 to $8,000.</strong> They're literally cutting the muscle that forward head posture has been tightening for free, every day, for years.</p>",
            "text_font": "body",
            "text_size": "subtext-md",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_line_height": 1.6,
            "text_letter_spacing": 0,
            "text_capitalize": false,
            "secondary_color": false,
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        }
      },
